Highway installs a user-provided abort handler by storing the function pointer in a function-local static `std::atomic<AbortFunc>`:

```cpp
std::atomic<AbortFunc>& AtomicAbortFunc() {
  static std::atomic<AbortFunc> func;
  return func;
}
```

The install function is:

```cpp
HWY_DLLEXPORT AbortFunc SetAbortFunc(AbortFunc func) {
  return AtomicAbortFunc().exchange(func);
}
```

So the installation is thread-safe because it uses `std::atomic<AbortFunc>::exchange`, atomically replacing the current handler with the new one.

Yes: swapping the handler hands back the previous one. `SetAbortFunc` returns the result of `exchange(func)`, i.e. the prior `AbortFunc`, or `nullptr` if none was registered. This is also documented in `hwy/base.h`:

```cpp
// Sets a new Abort() handler and returns the previous handler, which is nullptr
// if no previous handler was registered, and should otherwise be called from
// the new handler. If all handlers return, then Highway will terminate the app.
// Thread-safe.
HWY_DLLEXPORT AbortFunc SetAbortFunc(AbortFunc func);
```

At abort time, `Abort(...)` loads the currently installed handler atomically:

```cpp
AbortFunc handler = AtomicAbortFunc().load();
if (handler != nullptr) {
  handler(file, line, buf);
} else {
  fprintf(stderr, ...);
}
```

So: `SetAbortFunc` atomically swaps in the user handler and returns the previous handler.

