## How Highway installs a user-provided abort handler in a thread-safe way

### Files and symbols involved

| File | Symbols |
|---|---|
| `hwy/base.h` (lines 318–351) | `AbortFunc`, `WarnFunc`, `SetAbortFunc`, `SetWarnFunc`, `GetAbortFunc`, `GetWarnFunc` |
| `hwy/abort.cc` (lines 21–117) | `AtomicAbortFunc()`, `AtomicWarnFunc()`, `SetAbortFunc`, `SetWarnFunc`, `Abort`, `Warn` |

---

### The mechanism — step by step

#### 1. The handler pointer lives in a `std::atomic`

Inside an anonymous namespace in `hwy/abort.cc`, two file-local functions return references to **function-local `std::atomic` objects** (Meyer's singletons):

```cpp
std::atomic<AbortFunc>& AtomicAbortFunc() {
  static std::atomic<AbortFunc> func;   // zero-initialized → nullptr
  return func;
}
```

This is the canonical C++ way to get a lazily-initialized, thread-safe singleton. The `std::atomic` wrapper is what makes all reads and writes to the handler pointer lock-free and data-race-free.

#### 2. Installation uses `std::atomic::exchange` — the key to thread safety

```cpp
HWY_DLLEXPORT AbortFunc SetAbortFunc(AbortFunc func) {
  return AtomicAbortFunc().exchange(func);
}
```

`std::atomic::exchange` is a single indivisible read-modify-write operation (sequentially consistent by default). It atomically stores the new handler **and** returns the old one in one step, so no lock is needed and no race condition is possible even if two threads call `SetAbortFunc` simultaneously.

#### 3. Dispatch also uses an atomic load

When `Abort()` is actually called, it reads the handler with an atomic load:

```cpp
AbortFunc handler = AtomicAbortFunc().load();
if (handler != nullptr) {
    handler(file, line, buf);
} else {
    fprintf(stderr, ...);
}
```

This means a handler installed on one thread is immediately and safely visible to any thread that subsequently calls `Abort()`.

---

