### What it does
`escape(s: &str) -> String` turns a literal string into a safe glob pattern by **bracketing every glob meta-character** so it is treated as a literal. The meta-characters it handles are `?`, `*`, `[`, `]`, `{`, and `}` — each one is wrapped in `[…]` (e.g. `*` → `[*]`, `[` → `[[]`). The `!` character is intentionally left alone because it is only special *inside* brackets, not at the top level.

### Relevant source (from `crates/globset/src/lib.rs`)

```rust
/// Escape meta-characters within the given glob pattern.
///
/// The escaping works by surrounding meta-characters with brackets. For
/// example, `*` becomes `[*]`.
pub fn escape(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut escaped = String::with_capacity(s.len());
    for c in s.chars() {
        match c {
            // note that ! does not need escaping because it is only special
            // inside brackets
            '?' | '*' | '[' | ']' | '{' | '}' => {
                escaped.push('[');
                escaped.push(c);
                escaped.push(']');
            }
            c => {
                escaped.push(c);
            }
        }
    }
    escaped
}
```

